---
import PostLayout from "../layouts/PostLayout.astro";
---

<PostLayout
	title="Technical Background"
	description="How I got to work in software."
	metadata={false}
>
	<p>
		If it can serve any context, here are some words about how I got to work in
		software.
	</p>

	<p>
		I love sound. Most of all, I love how sound "sounds". Passed long nights of
		my adolescence messing with music production software, saturating and
		texturing things to creative places.
	</p>

	<p>
		This love for sound and music led me study electronics and
		telecommunications engineering at <a href="https://www.ua.pt/"
			>Universidade de Aveiro</a
		> in 2014.
	</p>

	<p>
		In my head, if I could make guitar pedals and studio equipment, how bad
		could telecommunications be.
	</p>

	<p>
		Turned out it was not that bad. I really liked the area, but fell in love
		with programming. My first vivid memory of loving programming was my first
		year at college. I had started programming a audio synthesizer in an <a
			href="https://www.terasic.com.tw/cgi-bin/page/archive.pl?Language=English&No=502"
			>Altera DE2-115 Education Board</a
		> and things began to click.
	</p>

	<p>
		Soon after, I began messing with microcontrollers. It was an university
		custom development board with a <a
			href="https://www.microchip.com/en-us/products/microcontrollers-and-microprocessors/32-bit-mcus/pic32-32-bit-mcus"
			>PIC32</a
		> MCU. I got really frustrated with the fact that everything I wanted to do with
		it had to be done using command via UART in my terminal. I wanted a user interface.
		How could I give any of this that I've been doing to someone else other than an
		engineer? It had to be another way.
	</p>

	<p>
		At the time, the way was programming a GUI in what I knew better, <a
			href="https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html">Matlab</a
		>. I was a big fan of it, but it was hard to distribute. All of us were
		running pirated versions of Matlab till half of the course, where we finally
		got access to student licenses. The problem was still the same, there was no
		way of giving it to anyone other that engineers.
	</p>

	<p>
		Eventually, in 2016, my second year at college, I starter seeing projects in
		my department, from our co-located computers and telematics students, which
		were using the web platform to do incredible things. That got me interest,
		so I started learning web technologies and build a web application with
		React for visualizing <a
			href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scattering_parameters">S-parameters</a
		> of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Touchstone_file"
			>TouchStone files</a
		>, to complement my frequent travels to the guided waves laboratory.
	</p>

	<p>
		My head was now in to programming. Electronics was really interesting, but
		the area perspectives were not great and programming was so freeing. I could
		do so much with my computer in my back and things moved really fast.
	</p>

	<p>
		At the end of college, I decided to go to Turin, in Italy, to study as an
		exchange student at <a href="https://www.polito.it">Politecnico di Torino</a
		>. There I could focus my studies on things that, otherwise, would be
		impossible in Aveiro in the electronics and telecommunications master's
		optional subjects. I got to study machine learning, automotive software and
		communications, IoT, distributed systems and cloud applications. Was here
		that I delve deep in programming projects in general. It was one of the most
		fulfilling times of my student life.
	</p>
</PostLayout>
